<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Statistical Interpretation of Entropy and the Second Law of Thermodynamics: The Underlying Explanation</title>
<metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
  <md:content-id>m42238</md:content-id>
  <md:title>Statistical Interpretation of Entropy and the Second Law of Thermodynamics: The Underlying Explanation</md:title>
  <md:abstract><list>
<item>Distinguish microstates from macrostates using the coin-toss and gas examples.</item>
<item>Compare macrostate probabilities by counting equally probable microstates.</item>
<item>Explain how microstates account for entropy and the tendency of isolated systems toward higher entropy.</item>
</list></md:abstract>
  <md:uuid>d9b9681a-d5d8-4640-91bd-68268ccebf33</md:uuid>
</metadata>

<content>

    <figure id="import-auto-id1169738148002"><media id="import-auto-id1169737715203" alt="Photograph of many coins laid down on a surface, some with heads shown up and some with tails shown up.">
        <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Figure_16_07_01.jpg" width="350"/>
      </media>

    <caption>When you toss a coin a large number of times, heads and tails tend to come up in roughly equal numbers. Why doesn’t heads come up 100, 90, or even 80% of the time? (credit: Jon Sullivan, PDPhoto.org)</caption></figure><para xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" id="import-auto-id1169737762485">A macroscopic state can correspond to many microscopic arrangements. Entropy increases with the number of microscopic arrangements compatible with that state. Spontaneous changes overwhelmingly favor macroscopic states compatible with more microscopic arrangements. Counting those arrangements provides a statistical explanation of the second law of thermodynamics.</para>
    <para xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" id="import-auto-id1169738082621">When you watch an emerging rain storm begin to wet the ground, you will notice that the drops fall in a disorganized manner both in time and in space. Some fall close together, some far apart, but you might never see them fall in straight, orderly rows. It is not impossible for rain to fall in an orderly pattern, just highly unlikely, because there are many more disorderly ways than orderly ones. To illustrate this fact, we will examine some random processes, starting with coin tosses.</para>

  <section id="fs-id1169737911144">
    <title>Coin Tosses</title>
    <para id="import-auto-id1169737818690">What are the possible outcomes of tossing 5 coins? Each coin can land either heads or tails. On the large scale, we are concerned only with the total heads and tails and not with the order in which heads and tails appear. The following possibilities exist:</para>
    <equation id="fs-id1169737756205" class="unnumbered">
      <m:math>
        <m:semantics>
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mrow>
              <m:mtable>
                <m:mtr>
                  <m:mrow>

                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mtext>5 heads, 0 tails</m:mtext>
                      </m:mrow>

                    <m:mrow/>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mtr>
                <m:mtr>
                  <m:mrow>

                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mtext>4 heads, 1 tail</m:mtext>
                      </m:mrow>

                    <m:mrow/>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mtr>
                <m:mtr>
                  <m:mrow>

                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mtext>3 heads, 2 tails</m:mtext>
                      </m:mrow>

                    <m:mrow/>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mtr>
                <m:mtr>
                  <m:mrow>

                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mtext>2 heads, 3 tails</m:mtext>
                      </m:mrow>

                    <m:mrow/>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mtr>
                <m:mtr>
                  <m:mrow>

                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mtext>1 head, 4 tails</m:mtext>
                      </m:mrow>

                    <m:mrow/>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mtr>
                <m:mtr>
                  <m:mrow>

                      <m:mrow>
                        <m:mtext>0 head, 5 tails</m:mtext>
                      </m:mrow>

                    <m:mrow/>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:mtr>
              </m:mtable>
              <m:mrow/>
            </m:mrow>
          </m:mrow>
          <m:annotation encoding="StarMath 5.0">alignl { stack {
 size 12{"5 heads, 0 tails"}  {} #
 size 12{"4 heads, 1 tail"}  {} #
 size 12{"3 heads, 2 tails"}  {} #
 size 12{"2 heads, 3 tails"}  {} #
 size 12{"1 head, 4 tails"}  {} #
 size 12{"0 head, 5 tails"}  {}
} } {}</m:annotation>
        </m:semantics>
      </m:math>
    </equation>
    <para xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" id="import-auto-id1169738137712">These are what we call macrostates. A <term id="import-auto-id1169738144323">macrostate</term> is a description of a system in terms of its macroscopic properties. It does not specify the details of the system, such as the order in which heads and tails occur or which coins are heads or tails.</para>
    <para id="import-auto-id1169737969172">Using this nomenclature, a system of 5 coins has the 6 possible macrostates just listed. Some macrostates are more likely to occur than others. For instance, there is only one way to get 5 heads, but there are several ways to get 3 heads and 2 tails, making the latter macrostate more probable. <link target-id="import-auto-id1169738164494"/> lists of all the ways in which 5 coins can be tossed, taking into account the order in which heads and tails occur. Each sequence is called a <term id="import-auto-id1169736609346">microstate</term>—a detailed description of every element of a system.</para>


 <table id="import-auto-id1169738164494" summary="A table with combinations of five coins tossed together five times is shown. The first column shows different toss results. The second column represents individual microstates, and the third column represents the number of microstates."><title>5-Coin Toss</title>
<tgroup cols="3"><colspec colnum="1" colname="c1"/>
        <colspec colnum="2" colname="c2"/>
        <colspec colnum="3" colname="c3"/>
        <thead><row>
            <entry/>
            <entry>Individual microstates</entry>
            <entry>Number of microstates</entry>
          </row></thead><tbody><row>
            <entry>5 heads, 0 tails</entry>
            <entry>HHHHH</entry>
            <entry align="center">1</entry>
          </row><row>
            <entry>4 heads, 1 tail</entry>
            <entry>HHHHT, HHHTH, HHTHH, HTHHH, THHHH</entry>
            <entry align="center">5</entry>
          </row><row>
            <entry>3 heads, 2 tails</entry>
            <entry>HTHTH, THTHH, HTHHT, THHTH, THHHT HTHTH, THTHH, HTHHT, THHTH, THHHT</entry>
            <entry align="center">10</entry>
          </row><row>
            <entry>2 heads, 3 tails</entry>
            <entry>TTTHH, TTHHT, THHTT, HHTTT, TTHTH, THTHT, HTHTT, THTTH, HTTHT, HTTTH</entry>
            <entry align="center">10</entry>
          </row><row>
            <entry>1 head, 4 tails</entry>
            <entry>TTTTH, TTTHT, TTHTT, THTTT, HTTTT</entry>
            <entry align="center">5</entry>
          </row><row>
            <entry>0 heads, 5 tails</entry>
            <entry>TTTTT</entry>
            <entry align="center">1</entry>
          </row><row>
            <entry/>
            <entry/>
            <entry align="center">Total: 32</entry>
          </row></tbody>


</tgroup>
</table><para xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" id="import-auto-id1169737980730">The macrostate of 3 heads and 2 tails can be achieved in 10 ways and is thus 10 times more probable than the one having 5 heads. Not surprisingly, it is equally probable to have the reverse, 2 heads and 3 tails. Similarly, it is equally probable to get 5 tails as it is to get 5 heads. Note that all of these conclusions are based on the crucial assumption that each microstate is equally probable. With coin tosses, this requires that the coins not be asymmetric in a way that favors one side over the other, as with loaded dice. For this counting argument, the accessible microstates must be equally probable.</para>
    <para xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" id="import-auto-id1169737927504">For five fair coins, the macrostates with all heads or all tails each have one microstate. Together, they account for only 2 of the 32 equally likely outcomes. The macrostates with three heads and two tails, or two heads and three tails, together have 20 microstates. They are therefore much more likely. Starting with five heads and tossing the coins again is likely to produce a macrostate compatible with more microstates. The reverse remains possible, but is less likely.</para>


